computing/bin/pythonlib/README

To generate the graphics of the outputs of various machine computations,
I first get a crude, ASCII output.  I then have a Python script that
converts the .out file to a .asy file, which is run through Asymptote
to get the .pdf that appears in the book.

The .asy files have an awkward way of getting to their common settings files.
That is, they all want to read src/asy/jh.asy to get things like common
function definitions, what colors to use, etc.  To read these they need the
relative path to the directory.  The Python file has to figure out the
common path.

It goes on the commandment in the INSTALL file that the top directory of the
installation has to be named from a small list of names: computing, toc, and
toc-master at the time this README is written.  Then there are some routines
to make the string that goes from where the .asy file is to where jh.asy is,
giving a string in the .asy file like "import("../../../src/asy");".

The routines used to figure out the common path have given me trouble, so I
localized them in a a library here, so the Finite State machine script, and
the Turing machine script, etc., can share them.